content:
anna: Quick sync — SDK surface for paginated endpoints across Py/TS/Go. Two axes: (A) ergonomic iterator/async-iterator first vs (B) explicit cursor object with resume token. Also need to decide retry semantics around page boundaries. Thoughts?
omar: +1 to explicit cursor for Go. for loops with a Resume() method map better than exposing channels/generators.
sylvia: TS team prefers async iterators (for-await-of). It's much nicer in user code than manual cursor.next(). But we can provide a Cursor wrapper that implements async iteration.
cheng: We should separate "surface ergonomics" from "transport semantics". Whether client exposes iterators or cursors shouldn't force a transport choice (http vs streaming). Keep transport adapter thin.
anna: Agree on decoupling. The debate is whether to standardize a single low-level primitive (fetchPage(cursor?) -> {items, next_token}) and build adapters for iterator styles in each language, or expose two competing high-level idioms.
omar: Single primitive + language adapters sounds pragmatic. Minimizes core surface tests. But watch out for retry behavior: if fetchPage is retried we may duplicate items unless backend guarantees stable pagination or idempotent tokens.
sylvia: Exactly. For async iterators, users expect transparent retries. If we do opaque tokens that advance server-side we need to commit to idempotency semantics or hold last-seen key.
cheng: Proposal: low-level primitive is fetch_page(params) -> {items, resume_token}. Transport layer will only retry idempotent operations (GET-like). For non-idempotent list endpoints we mark them non-retryable by default and provide configurable retry hooks.
anna: Can we add a small example of the JS ergonomic adapter to show mapping?
sylvia: Sure — rough draft:
```
// adapter.js
async function* iterateList(client, params) {
  let token = null;
  while (true) {
    const res = await client.fetchPage({ ...params, resume_token: token });
    for (const it of res.items) yield it;
    if (!res.resume_token) return;
    token = res.resume_token;
  }
}
```
This works but hides the retry surface — we need to document that fetchPage might throw on non-retryable pages.
omar: For Go, idiomatic would be:
```
cur := client.List(ctx, params)
for cur.Next() {
  item := cur.Item()
}
if err := cur.Err(); err != nil { /* handle */ }
```
Under the hood cur.Next() calls fetchPage and buffers items. If fetchPage fails mid-page we surface error and leave cur in a consistent state.
cheng: Also call out resumability: if a user saves resume_token and later calls List with resume_token we must guarantee server resumes. If not, the SDK should error fast.
anna: What about retries on transient errors during streaming transports? e.g., streaming responses over an HTTP chunked or websocket style where we lose connection mid-stream. Resume_token helps but only if server supported.
sylvia: Streams are great for latency but terrible for resume unless we have server-side offset tokens. I think we compress our options: recommend streaming only for non-paginated real-time feeds. For paginated listing use page-token + fetchPage.
omar: Implementation plan: 1) define low-level Page API (fetchPage signature + error classes), 2) implement adapters in each SDK (sync iterator for Go, async iterator for TS, generator for Py), 3) transport adapters implement retry policy for fetchPage only, 4) document idempotency expectations.
cheng: For retry policy config shape: minimal and consistent across SDKs: {maxAttempts, backoffMs, retryOn: [429,502], retryMode: 'idempotent-only'|'aggressive'}. Example:
```
client = new RedwoodClient({
  transport: 'http',
  retries: { maxAttempts: 3, backoffMs: 200, retryOn: [429, 502], retryMode: 'idempotent-only' }
});
```
This keeps API surface small and predictable.
anna: Edge case: what if fetchPage returns partial items due to backend paging while the token is still valid? Do we dedupe on client? That seems heavy. I prefer durability on the server side.
cheng: Agreed — server should guarantee page boundaries. We'll add a note in onboarding: server pagers must be stable across retries. If they cannot, mark non-retryable.
sylvia: One more ask: provide a lightweight Cursor interface in TS that exposes both manual next/resume and for-await-of. That would cover advanced users and keep ergonomic default.
omar: +1 to that pattern for consistency.
bot-deploy: reminder: RFC due next Tue. Please post a short spec in the rfc repo with examples for each language.
anna: I'll draft the spec: low-level fetch_page primitive, transport retry contract, adapter patterns for each language, and migration notes for older cursor API. Will ping for review tomorrow. :thumbsup:
